OK, here is what I want a notebook for.  I want to keep an inventory.
You know, the number of plusses on that Excalibur,  the types of rings,
the capabilities of my specialized armor, etc.  If I happen to stumble
across my own corpse in a future game, and in the midst of the loot I
find a "scroll of inventory", then reading it would tell me what all the
booty was.

Now here is how it would work:

1) You only find a scroll of inventory in the ghost-pile.  This scroll is
   never created by polymorph or blank scrolls.

2) A scroll of inventory is only created when a character does the following:
    o  Reads a spellbook of inventory
    o  Casts the spell successfully (this would be a high-level spell, too)

3) This creates a scroll of inventory.  The scroll has all normal scroll
   vulnerabilities (burning, etc.).  It must be carried with you when you die.
   If it gets burned or dropped before you die then it will be useless.  Put
   it in a bag of holding for safe keeping.  Think of it as a life-insurance
   policy that will help out the next guy who comes along.  ;^)

4) The adventurer who finds the loot and the scroll together should always
   bless the scroll before reading it (because inventory scrolls found in
   dead-men's graves are always cursed).  When read over the loot, each item
   the pile will be "named" (not identified).  If the scroll is cursed then
   the all the items will be identified as "good" items (ie an amulet of
   strangulation will be named "versus poison", etc).  If the scroll is
   uncursed then the item will be named correctly.  If the scroll is blessed
   then the name will also include plusses and/or minuses in the name.

Oh well, I think it would be fun.  ;^)

